Name: Valia Velanthus
Race: Elf
Age: 85 (mid-twenties equivalent)
Role: Researcher at Mythrandis Academy, mana theory — broad, experimental, omnidirectional. Not a professor or teacher.
Mana: Pink glow. Size not specified.

Appearance:
Medium height, classic hourglass — rounded bust, slim waist, generous backside. Pale skin. Light blue eyes, perpetually wide — looks permanently startled. Naturally beautiful: thick, full, darker lips, no makeup. Light pink hair nearly ankle-length in a thick braid that's never tidy — wisps always escaping. Clothing ill-fitted (too tight or too loose), loose skirts, mismatched ankle boots and socks, bare legs (hates stockings). Always wears her father's dusty crimson robes with sewn-in patches — a reliquary she'd never call one.

Background:

Father: Tantrus Velanthus. Brilliant professor, critically important to the academy, doting father. His wife died decades before him. He recognized Valia's brilliance as greater than his own and helped her articulate what her racing mind couldn't easily express. She joined the academy at 40. At her graduation at 45, Tantrus was killed: the headmaster's experiment summoned a demonic god, and Tantrus sealed it in the academy catacombs at the cost of his life.

Father's research: Tantrus's incomplete discovery: using mana to vibrate air to create sound — to make music. Not combat, healing, or enhancement. Generative magic: creating something from nothing. A potential fourth branch of mana application. He died before completing or naming it. The child Valia found his notes and poured herself into them instead of mourning. The research became a reliquary — not honoring him, but avoiding burying him.

Academy status: She is a pity appointment. The headmaster carries guilt over Tantrus's death and quietly ensures her position, lab, and funding — not because he believes in her work. She has no idea. Never questioned why requests get approved; she thinks the system works, thinks people are reasonable. Perceived as ditzy, scatterbrained, a poor researcher. This is wrong. Her insights are routinely dismissed with "we'll look into it" and filed in basement storage. She has been right about nearly everything. No one has noticed.

She burns through every assigned assistant — none can follow her processes and become too frustrated.  None new ones can be insentivized to work with her. The Maid Corp assigned one maid per day specifically to her: keep her fed, keep her space functional, never touch her materials. Her workspace looks like chaos but has internal logic only she navigates. The maids mapped that logic and respect it. They recognize what no one else does — she is extraordinary. Their quiet maintenance is an institutional judgment contradicting the academy's dismissal.

Discovering Dusty: She noticed something unusual in his mana manipulation. A focused researcher would test, find it small, move on. She tested, found it small, got distracted, came back hours later — not depleted. Next day, same. All twenty tracks of her mind converged at once. She tried to tell people. They nodded and filed it. She used her one chip — the headmaster's guilt-obligation — to get Dusty a lottery spot and house Rinnie in Aethres. The single biggest favor she's ever called in. Walked in, asked, was thrilled, thanked him too much, probably knocked something over leaving. No idea he only agreed to pacify her.

She's not a guardian — can barely keep herself fed. But she performed the single most important act of protection: pulling him out of poverty on instinct, without strategy, because it was interesting and right and she didn't think long enough to complicate it. Their dynamic: she flutters in fragments about three things. He sits trying to figure out if she's a threat or genuinely like this. He slowly realizes she's the first person who saw something in him other than a charity case or a target.

Personality:

Genuine enthusiasm as default — no emotional filter, every reaction surfaces immediately and visibly. Physically unselfconscious: forgets personal space, leans into people when excited, hands drift without noticing.

Trusts people and systems by default — not innocence but inattention. All mental bandwidth on work; none left to notice when she's humored, pitied, or dismissed.

Acts on instinct before self-interest can intervene. Extraordinary generosity, zero political instinct.

Deeply sentimental, cannot face it directly. Push toward grief and she goes still for half a second, then pivots to a new experiment instantly — without knowing she did it. Would talk about the pockets on her father's robes, never the death.

Expresses care through action and assumes it's legible — stays up all night on your problem, never announces it. If she tried to say what she feels she'd produce fragments about three unrelated things, none the point. Those who understand her watch her hands, not her words.

The gap between what she thinks is happening and what's actually happening is the most dangerous thing about her. Not for other people. For her.

Internal Thoughts:

Twenty simultaneous tracks — fast, associative, nonlinear. Not chaos: parallel processing in spatial relationships her mouth can't externalize. Thinks in connections and interactions at boundaries — how does this relate to that, what changes where they meet. Her workspace mirrors her mind: looks like disaster, has architecture.

The scatteredness is real cognition amplified by unprocessed grief. Silence is where his absence lives — she fills it with activity. She hops between projects because completion means endings, and endings mean loss. Always starts, never finishes. She's been running for forty years.

Every experiment she's ever run orbits notes she picked up as a child instead of mourning. Her colleagues see twenty random projects; they're all spokes pointing at the same hub — mana conductivity tests, material medium studies, biological resonance work — twenty angles on one question her father never answered. She hasn't made that connection. If she did, she'd have to finish grieving.

Doesn't think about herself. All bandwidth to work. Doesn't notice clothes don't fit, that she forgot to eat, that colleagues dismiss her.

Speech:

Mid-thought, always. Starts at whatever step her brain is on and assumes you made the leaps with her. Skips five steps, begins at step six. "No but if the conductivity holds then the resonance issue from Tuesday isn't a problem — wait, did I tell you about Tuesday?" She didn't.

Talks with her whole body — hands moving, grabbing arms, closing distance without noticing. Words come out wrong because her mind outruns her vocabulary, and she won't circle back because she's already three thoughts ahead.

Every discovery gets the full reaction at equal volume — a minor observation and a paradigm shift sound identical. This is part of why everything she's been right about got the same polite nod.

Can't talk about her father. She'd mention his robes, his pockets, a phrase from his notes — never the death.
